{
  "beruf": "elektro-technik",
  "title": "Private security: guarding and access control",
  "level": "A2–B1",
  "reviewed": false,
  "_note": "french-for-work run, 2026-11-13: written by hand for France, not yet checked by a second reader. translations.en = English meaning; deHint = German translation (the 'German' tab). Words already in the lexicon are refs, with this job's own example and sense. Working conditions are general orientation only.",
  "intro": "Private security is a common job for newcomers in France: shops, offices, events, car parks, building sites and fire safety. This deck has the equipment, what you do on a round, the shift and its procedures, safety and emergencies, and how to talk with your team and the public. Say vous to visitors and clients, always; colleagues often say tu.",
  "introEasy": "Do you work in security in France? Learn the words for your work here.",
  "jobs": [
    "Agent de sécurité",
    "Agent de prévention et de sécurité",
    "Agent SSIAP",
    "Agent de surveillance (magasin, événement)",
    "Agent cynophile / maître-chien"
  ],
  "tipps": [
    "Always say vous to the public and stay polite and calm: « Bonjour, puis-je voir votre badge ? » « Pouvez-vous patienter un instant ? » With colleagues, tu is common.",
    "Your job is mostly to watch, to check and to report. Write down what you saw and tell your chef de poste: « Je préviens mon chef de poste. » In an emergency, call the right number first (18, 15 or 17).",
    "At the handover, say what happened and what is still open: « Rien à signaler. » or « Il y a eu une alarme à vingt-deux heures. » Notes go in the main courante or the rapport.",
    "Security work usually needs a professional card (carte professionnelle, issued by the CNAPS) and a training such as the CQP APS or TFP APS; fire safety agents have SSIAP levels. What an agent may do at a bag check or a body search depends on strict rules and on the employer's instructions, so never assume you have the right: ask your employer, France Travail or a union. This is general orientation, not legal advice."
  ],
